Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

REQ-CONDUCTOR-FIXGATE-1. Live specimen, 2026-08-23: the conductor's
test-fixer, told 'fix the failing tests' and 'do NOT modify
scripts/research_conductor.py', complied by adding pytest.mark.skipif to
the failing (UNTRACKED) test file and reverting the foreign block the
tests covered — reason string 'this task explicitly forbids modifying
that file'. The suite went green; the repair was indistinguishable from
erasure. Measured: the standing suite already carries ~93 skip markers,
so this is ongoing erosion, not a one-off; this gate covers NEW erasure
only and the standing debt is recorded in the spec.

Mechanism: snapshot the task's edits before the fix loop (dirty tracked
files + untracked tests/ files — the specimen's file was untracked, and
a tracked-diff-only gate would have missed it); after each fix attempt,
detect added skip markers and dirty->clean reversions; on detection,
RESTORE the paths from the snapshot, write a durable BLOCK line, and
discard the attempt — the suite is never run over a skip-poisoned tree,
because its green result would be the lie the gate exists to prevent.
Fail closed: a fix the gate cannot audit is not accepted. The fixer
prompt now names the forbidden repairs.

Sibling hardening, same incident: the self-edit revert (research_
conductor.py guard that checkouts foreign edits to its own source — it
destroyed this session's in-flight block three times) now rescues the
diff to ops/.conductor_selfedit_rescue/<ts>.patch and writes a durable
WARN before reverting.

The gate's own suite caught a real bug before landing: the restore
branch deleted a tracked-but-clean test file instead of checking it
out. Fixed; the incident is named in the code comment.

Takes effect at the running conductor's next restart (or its own
fresh-exec once this lands in HEAD).

